# Where the dashboard lives — and how to turn it on

The dashboard is now a real page in your site instead of a loose file. It reads
**live from Supabase**, so it always shows whatever months are loaded — no
regenerating.

- **URL:** `https://<your-netlify-site>/admin/analytics.html`
- **How it works:** the page calls a small server function (`/.netlify/functions/analytics`)
  that queries the `monthly_sales` table with your secret service-role key (kept on the
  server, never in the browser) and returns the totals. A **Period** dropdown at the top
  right switches between *All periods*, *January 2026*, *February 2026*, and each future
  month you load.

## Deploy it (3 files, already in the repo)
1. `netlify/functions/analytics.js` — the server function
2. `public/admin/analytics.html` — the dashboard page
3. `supabase/load_feb2026_sales.sql` — February data (run in Supabase)

Commit the two code files with your normal deploy. Netlify picks up the function
automatically (it's in `netlify/functions/`).

## Set the environment variables (Netlify → Site settings → Environment variables)
- `SUPABASE_URL` = `https://ycqmztthwldytkzyvmiv.supabase.co`
- `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE` = **your secret service-role key** (Supabase → Project Settings
  → API → `service_role`). This is the secret one — it goes here, never in chat or in the
  page. The function reads it server-side.
- `ANALYTICS_TOKEN` *(optional but recommended)* = any passcode you choose. If set, the
  page asks for it before showing numbers, so the URL isn't wide open. Leave it unset
  while testing.

After saving env vars, trigger a redeploy so the function picks them up.

## Load February
In the Supabase SQL Editor, run **`load_feb2026_sales.sql`** (idempotent, safe to re-run).
It loads all 529 February rows and ends with a per-line summary. Once it's in, the
dashboard's Period dropdown will show February automatically.

